<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revestment Act 1765</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Isle of Man Purchase Act 1765</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revestment Act of 1765 was the law by which the British government bought the sovereign rights over the Isle of Man from the Duke and Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a> for £70,000. Its aim was to stop smuggling through the island, which cost the Treasury heavily in lost customs duties. The lordship passed to the crown, and Man became a crown dependency.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The feudal rights over the Isle of Man had been conferred by the Assurance of the Isle of Man Act 1609 on William Stanley, 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, an ancestor of the Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>, and on his heirs, so that a further act of Parliament was needed to end them. The authority for the Treasury to contract for their purchase came from sections 25 and 26 of the Customs, etc., Revenues Act 1725, which was passed in 1726. For a time there had also been plans to merge the island into the English county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>, which met fierce resistance from the inhabitants, led by Sir George Moore, then Speaker of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-keys">House of Keys</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act, also known as the Act of Revestment, was passed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a> to buy the feudal rights that the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a> held as Lords of Man and to revest them in the British Crown. It gave effect to an earlier contract between Charlotte, Duchess of Atholl, and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, represented by the Treasury, for the sale of those rights for £70,000. It came into force with the royal assent on 10 May 1765, and the payment to the duchess was to be made no later than 1 June 1765. The merger with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> was not carried out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholls</a> kept their manorial rights, the patronage of the bishopric and certain other perquisites. These were sold in 1828 for £417,144, more than £20,000,000 in modern terms, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-atholl">Duke of Atholl</a>'s Rights, Isle of Man Act 1825. The act of 1765 remained law for more than two centuries, until the Statute Law (Repeals) Act 1976 repealed it in full, with effect from 27 May 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isle of Man Purchase Act 1765” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British crown bought the sovereign rights of the Lordship of Mann from the Duke of Atholl through the <b>Revestment Act</b> of 1765, mainly to stop smuggling that cost the Treasury duties.</blockquote><a href="/state/isle-of-man">Isle of Man</a></li><li><blockquote>To end smuggling, the British government bought the sovereign rights with the <b>Revestment Act</b> of 1765, creating the Crown Dependency of the Isle of Man.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
